Make libopaque's hand-written src/makefile survive a cross-compile, and have it
install one plainly-named shared library.

libopaque has no configure step; build.sh just runs `make` and `make install`
inside src/ with forge's cross CC/AR/CFLAGS/LDFLAGS in the environment. The
upstream makefile is written for a native Linux or macOS build, and four things
have to give.

  1. `LDFLAGS=-g $(LIBS)` is a plain `=`, so it discards whatever LDFLAGS forge
     exported -- the cross sysroot and the -L paths pointing at the host deps
     (flet-libsodium, flet-liboprf) never reach the link line. Changed to `+=`,
     which for a variable inherited from the environment appends to that value
     rather than replacing it.

  2. The `uname -s` / `uname -m` block describes the BUILD machine, never the
     target, so every branch of it is wrong here. On a macOS build host it
     takes the Darwin arm: SOEXT=dylib plus an -install_name pointing into the
     build tree -- wrong for Android, and wrong on iOS too, where
     serious-python only converts files named `*.so` into frameworks. On a
     Linux build host it takes the else arm: GNU/ELF-only link flags
     (-Wl,-z,defs / relro / noexecstack / now) and -fcf-protection /
     -fstack-clash-protection chosen from the host's `uname -m`, none of which
     suit the NDK or Apple toolchains on the arm slices. The whole block is
     commented out rather than rewritten, which is why hunk 1 also has to add
     `SOEXT=so`: with the block gone nothing else ever assigns SOEXT and the
     makefile would emit `libopaque.` with an empty extension. A side effect is
     that SOFLAGS stays empty, so no -soname / -install_name is baked in.

  3. `all:` no longer builds `tests utils/opaque $(MANPAGES)`. The test
     binaries and the `opaque` CLI are target executables that nothing here can
     run, and MANPAGES is gated on the build host happening to have pandoc
     installed. Only the two libraries are wanted in the wheel.

  4. The install rule wrote `libopaque.so.0` and then symlinked
     `libopaque.so -> $(DESTDIR)$(PREFIX)/lib/libopaque.so.0`, an absolute path
     into the build tree. Neither half survives packaging: a wheel is a zip,
     and on Android only files named `lib*.so` are surfaced through jniLibs, so
     a `.so.0` is invisible and the symlink's target does not exist on the
     device. `cp $< $@` installs one real file under the plain name -- which is
     exactly the name the consumer looks for, since
     recipes/opaque/patches/mobile.patch falls back to
     `ctypes.cdll.LoadLibrary('libopaque.so')` when find_library() comes up
     empty. build.sh relies on the same name (it moves `$PREFIX/lib/
     libopaque.so` to the site-packages root on iOS).

Provenance: added by "libsodium, libopaque, liboprf" (PR #28, Jan 2025) with no
written rationale, and untouched since -- it still applied verbatim across the
0.99.8 -> 1.0.1 bump in PR #68. Point 1 is unambiguous from the makefile
itself; points 2-4 are read off the diff together with what build.sh and the
opaque consumer recipe do with the result.

